(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_mechanicalpropertiesofsolids=[{id:"neet-2026-q018",pn:18,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Solids",st:"Elastic moduli",q:`Match List I with List II:
A. Young's Modulus \u2014 I. (\u0394d/\u0394L)(L/d)
B. Compressibility \u2014 II. FL/(A(\u0394L))
C. Bulk Modulus \u2014 III. \u2212(1/\u0394P)(\u0394V/V)
D. Poisson's Ratio \u2014 IV. \u2212P(V/\u0394V)
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],c:3,s:`A. Young's modulus = stress/strain = FL/(A\u0394L), which is II.
B. Compressibility = 1/(bulk modulus) = \u2212(1/\u0394P)(\u0394V/V), which is III.
C. Bulk modulus = \u2212P(V/\u0394V), which is IV.
D. Poisson's ratio = lateral strain/longitudinal strain = (\u0394d/\u0394L)(L/d), which is I.
So A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I.`},{id:"neet-2024-q008",pn:8,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Solids",st:"Elastic limit and Young's modulus",q:"The maximum elongation of steel wire of 1 m length if the elastic limit of steel and its Young's modulus, respectively, are 8 \xD7 10\u2078 N m\u207B\xB2 and 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2, is-",o:["40 mm","8 mm","4 mm","0.4 mm"],c:2,s:`Elongation is maximum when the stress equals the elastic limit.
\u03B4max = (\u03C3 \xD7 L)/Y = (8 \xD7 10\u2078 \xD7 1)/(2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9)
\u03B4max = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m = 4 mm.`},{id:"neet-2024-q042",pn:42,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Solids",st:"Thermal stress",q:"A metallic bar of Young\u2019s modulus, 0.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2 and coefficient of linear thermal expansion 10\u207B\u2075 \xB0C\u207B\xB9, length 1 m and area of cross-section 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB2 is heated from 0\xB0C to 100\xB0C without expansion or bending. The compressive force developed in it is:",o:["100 \xD7 10\xB3 N","2 \xD7 10\xB3 N","5 \xD7 10\xB3 N","50 \xD7 10\xB3 N"],c:3,s:`Longitudinal strain = thermal strain = \u03B1\u0394T.
Strain \u03B4 = 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 10\xB2 = 10\u207B\xB3.
Compressive stress = \u03B4 \xD7 Y = 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 0.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 = 0.5 \xD7 10\u2078 N m\u207B\xB2.
Compressive force = stress \xD7 area = 0.5 \xD7 10\u2078 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 0.5 \xD7 10\u2075 N.
This is 5 \xD7 10\u2074 \xD7 (10/10) = 50 \xD7 10\xB3 N.`},{id:"neet-2023-q016",pn:16,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Solids",st:"Stress",q:"Let a wire be suspended from the ceiling (rigid support) and stretched by a weight W attached at its free end. The longitudinal stress at any point of cross-sectional area A of the wire is:",o:["Zero","2W/A","W/A","W/2A"],c:2,s:`Longitudinal stress = internal restoring force/area.
The restoring force equals the external force W.
So stress = W/A.`,sfig:{w:200,h:200,alt:"Wire hanging from a rigid ceiling with a block W at its end: W acts downward and tension T upward on the wire.",d:[["l",50,20,150,20],["l",56,20,50,12],["l",66,20,60,12],["l",76,20,70,12],["l",86,20,80,12],["l",96,20,90,12],["l",106,20,100,12],["l",116,20,110,12],["l",126,20,120,12],["l",136,20,130,12],["l",146,20,140,12],["l",100,20,100,100],["l",100,100,100,60,"a"],["t",108,76,"T"],["r",80,100,40,36],["t",100,123,"W","m"],["l",100,136,100,178,"a"],["t",100,192,"W","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2022-q040",pn:40,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Solids",st:"Shear modulus and elasticity of materials",q:"Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R). Assertion (A): The stretching of a spring is determined by the shear modulus of the material of the spring. Reason (R): A coil spring of copper has more tensile strength than a steel spring of same dimensions. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below",o:["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)"],c:1,s:`When a coil spring is stretched, its length and volume do not really change; only its shape changes.
So stretching is decided by the shear modulus. (A) is true.
Steel has more tensile strength than copper. So (R) is false.`},{id:"neet-2020-q126",pn:126,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Solids",st:"Young's modulus",q:"A wire of length L, area of cross section A is hanging from a fixed support. The length of the wire changes to L\u2081 when mass M is suspended from its free end. The expression for Young's modulus is :",o:["Mg(L\u2081\u2013L)/(AL)","MgL/(AL\u2081)","MgL/(A(L\u2081\u2013L))","MgL\u2081/(AL)"],c:2,s:`Young's modulus Y = stress/strain = (F/A)/(\u0394L/L).
F = Mg and the extension \u0394L = L\u2081 \u2212 L.
Y = MgL/(A(L\u2081 \u2212 L)).`},{id:"neet-2019-q131",pn:131,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Solids",st:"Elastic potential energy",q:"When a block of mass M is suspended by a long wire of length L, the length of the wire becomes (L + l). The elastic potential energy stored in the extended wire is -",o:["\xBD Mgl","\xBD MgL","Mgl","MgL"],c:0,s:`The load goes down by l, so gravity does Mgl of work.
The force grows slowly from 0 to Mg as the wire stretches, so only half of this goes into the wire.
Elastic energy U = \xBD Mgl.`,sfig:{w:140,h:250,alt:"A wire hangs from a fixed support with block M at its lower end; a downward arrow marked l shows the extension of the wire.",d:[["l",15,12,100,12,""],["l",55,12,55,128,"B"],["l",45,128,65,128,""],["l",55,128,55,145,""],["r",33,145,46,30,""],["l",33,149,37,145,""],["l",33,155,43,145,""],["l",33,161,49,145,""],["l",33,167,55,145,""],["l",33,173,61,145,""],["l",37,175,67,145,""],["l",43,175,73,145,""],["l",49,175,79,145,""],["l",55,175,79,151,""],["l",61,175,79,157,""],["l",67,175,79,163,""],["l",73,175,79,169,""],["t",13,164,"M","m"],["l",92,162,110,162,""],["l",101,162,101,238,"a"],["t",109,200,"l",""]]}},{id:"neet-2018-q010",pn:10,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Solids",st:"Young's modulus and stretching",q:"Two wires are made of the same material and have the same volume. The first wire has cross-sectional area A and the second wire has cross-sectional area 3A. If the length of the first wire is increased by \u0394l on applying a force F, how much force is needed to stretch the second wire by the same amount ?",o:["9 F","6 F","4 F","F"],c:0,s:`Y = (F/A)/(\u0394l/l), so \u0394l = (Fl)/(AY).
Same volume V = Al, so l = V/A and \u0394l = (FV)/(A\xB2Y).
For wire 1: \u0394l = (FV)/(A\xB2Y). For wire 2 (area 3A): \u0394l = (F\u2032V)/(9A\xB2Y).
Equal \u0394l gives F\u2032 = 9F.`},{id:"neet-2017-q167",pn:167,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Solids",st:"Bulk modulus",q:"The bulk modulus of a spherical object is 'B'. If it is subjected to uniform pressure 'p', the fractional decrease in radius is :",o:["p/(3B)","p/B","B/(3p)","3p/B"],c:0,s:`Bulk modulus B = p/(\u0394V/V), so \u0394V/V = p/B.
For a sphere V \u221D r\xB3, so \u0394V/V = 3\u0394r/r.
Therefore \u0394r/r = p/(3B).`}]})();
